Part A — Creating Data Frames
# TODO: Create three vectors of equal length (5 students):
# name (character): "Ana", "Ben", "Cid", "Dex", "Eli"
# age (numeric): 20, 21, 19, 22, 20
# passed (logical): TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE
```{r}
# Create a character vector for student names
name <- c("Ana", "Ben", "Cid", "Dex", "Eli")

# Create a numeric vector for student ages
age <- c(20, 21, 19, 22, 20)

# Create a logical vector for passing status
passed <- c(TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE)

# Display the vectors
print(name)
print(age)
print(passed)
```

# TODO: Combine name, age, and passed into a data frame called students
# using data.frame(name = name, age = age, passed = passed)
# Print students
```{r}
# Create a data frame
students <- data.frame(
  name = name,
  age = age,
  passed = passed
)

# Display the students data frame
print(students)
```
Answer:The data frame named students was successfully created with five students and three columns: name, age, and passed. Each row represents one student and their corresponding information.


