Question #3:students["age"] and students$age both seem to give you the age column, but class() reveals they are different types of objects.
Run class() on each and explain the difference, connecting it to what you learned about single vs. double brackets on lists in a previous lab.
```{r}
# Check the class using single brackets
class(students["age"])

# Check the class using the dollar sign
class(students$age)

# Check the class using double brackets
class(students[["age"]])
```
Answer:Using single brackets students["age"] returns a data frame containing the age column. On the other hand, using the dollar sign $ or double brackets [[]] returns the actual age vector, which is numeric. This is similar to lists in R because single brackets return a smaller list, while double brackets extract the actual element.

Question #4:In df-multiple-conditions, explain what the & operator is doing to the two logical vectors (students$passed == TRUE and students$age > 19) before that combined result is used to filter rows.
What would change if you used | (OR) instead of & (AND)?

Answer:The & operator checks both conditions for every student. It returns TRUE only when the student passed and is older than 19. If I use the | operator instead, it will return TRUE when at least one condition is satisfied. This means students who passed or are older than 19 will be included in the result, even if they do not meet both conditions.

